// @no-instantiate expected i32
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A lambda that captures an INFERENCE-HOLE parameter and indexes it,
// where the call site pins the element to a non-i32 scalar: `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and the
// module does not validate — `type mismatch: expected i32, found f64`.
//
// THREE CONTROLS, in `closures/lambda-captures-inferred-elem-controls.vl`, and between them the
// trigger is exact:
//   · the same program called with an i32 list — compiles, runs;
//   · the same f64 list with the index moved OUT of the lambda — compiles, runs;
//   · the same lambda with the parameter ANNOTATED `f64[]` — compiles, runs.
// So it is the pair: an un-annotated (hole) parameter whose element the call site pins to a
// non-i32 scalar, read through a CAPTURE.
//
// The capture cell is laid out before monomorphization has pinned the hole, so it takes the
// default i32 slot; the pinned re-check then types `self[0]` as `f64` and stores an f64 into it.
// The non-captured read works because it is emitted after the pin, from the param slot directly.
//
// FOUND BY A REVIEW LOOKING SOMEWHERE ELSE. It came out of witnesses built to attack the
// inferred-return ROLLBACK (B76) — programs shaped to make a `recordInferRet` fire inside
// `monoInferListElem`'s speculative re-check. That probe is unreachable, so the witnesses proved
// what they were built to prove by being byte-identical on both sides; four of them also failed to
// validate on master, identically, which is how this surfaced. **The witness population built to
// test one thing is a corpus for everything else it happens to contain** — worth remembering,
// because these shapes are exactly the ones `tests/cases` lacks: nobody writes them on purpose.
function f(self) {
  const g = () => {
    const z = self[0]
    return z
  }
  print(g())
  return self.length
}

print(f([1.5, 2.5]))
